WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.332
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:03.610
- Hello.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:07.235
In this video, I'm going to
be talking about webR 0.2.0,

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:11.450
and, in particular, R
packages for WebAssembly.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:15.770
So webR supports
loading R packages.

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:19.550
They have to first be available
on the virtual file system.

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:22.850
But once they're there,
they can be loaded.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:26.990
So there's a few ways to
ensure that those packages are there.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:30.500
They can be manually copied
to the virtual file system

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.435
location, /usr/lib/R/library.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:35.930
But there's also a
helper function provided by webR

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:40.730
that will install R packages
to that location for you.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.780
This helper function also
is connected to a binary

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.280
repository that contains

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:50.390
R packages built
for WebAssembly.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:53.780
So if you ask for a package
that exists in that repository,

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.240
it will go and download
it, extract the files,

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.850
and put them on the virtual
file system for you.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.610
There is

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:05.990
issues when it comes to

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:09.290
converting R packages
to run on WebAssembly.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.590
The biggest issue is probably
that R packages with compiled

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:16.970
code need to have that code
recompiled so that it can run

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:18.260
under WebAssembly.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.420
And that includes not only the
code that exists inside an R

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.030
package but also any system
libraries that that code

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:30.845
depends on. So if you have some
C code in an R package that uses

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:32.945
libxml2, for example,

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:35.930
to pass some XML files,

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:39.440
that system library,
that XML system library,

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:43.790
will also have to be
converted into WebAssembly and provided

00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:46.700
so that that package
can run under webR.

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:51.060
So we've done some
work in webR 0.2.0

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.685
to increase the number of system
libraries available for use with webR.

00:01:56.630 --> 00:02:00.230
And the way we do this
is that we have certain recipes that

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:05.180
exist in the webR source
repository. And if you ask for

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:06.890
these packages to be built,

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:08.870
there's a command you can
run to build them all.

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:13.040
It will go away and build
all these packages one by one

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:15.620
for WebAssembly.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:18.500
And once those packages
are built for WebAssembly,

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:22.085
R packages can be
linked to use them

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.725
to include that code
in those R packages.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.260
So there's a selection
of new libraries here.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.400
There's various graphics
libraries that are used in the

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.005
previous videos when I talked
about the cairo bitmap graphics.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:40.250
So there's

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.450
text rendering and
graphics libraries.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:49.020
There's some various
utility libraries. There's sqlite3

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:51.120
for databases.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.260
And there's also some
geospatial and computational

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:58.500
geometry packages, like
geos, gdal, and proj.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:03.780
And they are used to
enable some extra geospatial packages

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.370
in the latest version of webR.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.760
By including these
system libraries,

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.560
we've been able to
expand the number of R

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.140
packages that are
supported by webR.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:18.690
And those R packages are
hosted on a binary repository

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:19.920
at that URL there.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.770
And this is the
repository that's automatically used by

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:26.610
the webR install command if you do
ask for an R package in webR.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.480
So in the previous release,

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:31.850
by the end of that
release cycle,

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:35.165
we had about 219 packages
available for use.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:38.228
Now we have more.
We have 10,324,

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:41.750
which is about 51% of CRAN.

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:45.740
And that's how many packages are
available in that binary repo now.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:50.630
I should say that we
haven't tested all of these packages.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:54.710
What available here means is that
a package is able to be

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:58.370
compiled by the
Emscripten compiler for WebAssembly.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.050
So that R package
can be compiled

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:03.830
as well as all the packages
that it depends on.

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:07.790
So if an R package depends
on some other R package, if it's

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:09.560
available in webR,

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:13.370
it means that all of
that dependency chain has been

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:16.579
resolved and all of those
packages have been compiled for WebAssembly.

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:19.209
So

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:22.610
because there are quite a
few packages available now,

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:25.550
you can use built-in R tools
to list those packages.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:29.490
I think the function is
available.packages to list all

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.920
of those. But it might
be a bit tricky to

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:35.490
run through all that data,

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:38.340
particularly if you just want to know
if a single package works or not.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.700
So one of the things we've
done is we've updated the repository

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:44.220
so that the index page--

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:47.190
if you were to visit that
repository in a web browser,

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:50.700
the index page that you
see after a loading period looks

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:53.370
like this. And it tells
you how many packages are available,

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:55.710
and it gives you a list
that you can search through.

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:58.620
So, for example, if you wanted
to know if ggplot is available,

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:02.730
you could just type ggplot2 in the
box there. And you can see, OK, package

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:07.890
ggplot2 is available and all
of its dependencies are also

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:10.620
available. So I can use
that package in webR.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:14.640
So this is a really nice
useful little tool to just see which

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.200
packages are now
available for use in webR.

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:25.400
Some examples of packages
that are now available. tidyverse

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.890
and tidymodels are
both available.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:31.550
So if I run that code,
it will go and download those packages.

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:34.940
There they are.

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.330
Not all of the
tidyverse packages are available,

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:43.520
I should admit. There are some
that can't easily be used in

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:46.925
WebAssembly, because they need to
connect to the network, and

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:50.930
direct network access is one
of the things that's restricted in

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:52.550
the WebAssembly environment.

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:56.570
But the core tidyverse packages
are there and can be attached.

00:05:56.570 --> 00:05:57.890
And the same with tidymodels.

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:01.010
The tidymodels packages are available
and they can all be

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:04.760
attached. So you can run
your tidymodels code in webR.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:08.450
And you can run your
core tidyverse code in webR.

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:12.800
Geospatial packages are
available. So, in particular,

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:15.455
that includes the sf
package for simple features.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:17.850
So if I run this
piece of code again,

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:20.475
it will go and download
those packages and then run

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:25.710
this small piece of code that's going
to plot a map for us projected

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:29.100
onto a certain type
of map projection.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:32.385
So there's some computational
geometry going on there.

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:36.840
And then all of those
state lines are plotted there with

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:40.035
ggplot here in this example.
So there are various

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:44.520
geospatial data packages
available now to use in webR.

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:48.300
And there's also
database packages.

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:52.470
So here is an example of
the DBI package being used with the

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:53.640
RSQLite package.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:56.415
Database systems

00:06:58.140 --> 00:06:59.670
on a server somewhere connected

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:02.190
over the network won't work,

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:04.440
again because network
connections are one of the

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:06.360
things in WebAssembly
that's restricted.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:10.920
But in-memory databases, like
SQLite, is available. So here's

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:15.990
an example that takes some data
and puts it into an SQLite database.

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:19.560
And then we have some SQL code
here that we can use to query

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:22.725
that data. So if you're a
user who prefers to use R

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:25.635
with DBI to run SQL code,

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:27.660
you can do that.

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:30.375
So you can change that code. Well,

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:34.590
maybe I can--

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:36.570
there we go.

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:41.370
You can change the code and
get immediate results in webR.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:45.360
You can see I'm not a SQL
programmer, as you can see by

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.925
that previous example.
But it does work.

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:51.900
You're able to run your
SQL code directly there. Again,

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.880
SQLite is probably the only
database that is well supported here,

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:00.150
just because many other
databases will require network connections.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:03.330
And, finally,

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:06.630
the last thing I want to
talk about with R packages is Shiny

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:08.430
and httpuv,

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:10.920
which is one of the
support packages that Shiny uses.

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:13.725
So the httpuv package,

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:16.380
again, is based on
network connections.

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:19.640
It's a server that can
listen for httpuv--

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:20.750
sorry--
HTTP

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:25.550
and WebSocket traffic, and
then respond to those requests.

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:30.200
So a package shim has
been created for webR,

00:08:32.090 --> 00:08:33.860
which is available
on the on the r-wasm

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:37.250
organization if you want to
read the source code for that.

00:08:37.970 --> 00:08:41.600
And what that is is it's a
package that acts like httpuv

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:45.380
but is re-implemented to be
used with webR. And, in particular,

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:47.480
what it's doing is
taking advantage of a JavaScript

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:49.010
feature known as
service workers.

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:54.080
And service workers are nice because
they can act as network proxies. So,

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:55.240
normally,

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:59.900
traffic that httpuv would
handle over the network is

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:03.710
instead being handled entirely
client side inside a JavaScript

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:04.970
service worker instead.

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:09.320
And that service worker acts
as our network server, when,

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:11.150
really, it's all
running client side.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:15.830
With this shim, Shiny apps are
able to run under webR, and,

00:09:15.830 --> 00:09:18.035
in particular, they can
run entirely client side.

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:23.040
So the way this works is
that the R Shiny server software

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:27.735
thinks it's talking to httpuv
through its normal R package API.

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:29.760
And at the other side,

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:32.370
we have the Shiny web
client that the user is interacting

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:35.940
with. That thinks it's
talking over the network

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:39.690
to the Shiny server, but
instead that is being

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:42.240
intercepted, as I say, by the
JavaScript service worker.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:47.040
And then that proxy in the
middle between the Shiny server

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:47.955
and the Shiny client

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:51.600
converts those network
requests, captures them,

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:53.580
converts them, and
sends them to webR.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:58.725
And webR works with
httpuv to handle those messages.

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:01.200
And the responses
from Shiny, again,

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:05.190
are sent back to the client
at the front end through

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:09.240
that network proxy, through
that service worker. So

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:10.785
through this

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:13.170
package shimming process,

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:15.180
we're able to run

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:19.030
Shiny apps entirely
within the browser,

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:22.480
with both the front-end and
the backend server all running

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:24.355
inside the JavaScript worker.

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:28.270
This is a similar
scheme, by the way,

00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:30.700
to how Shinylive
for Python works.

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:33.280
It's not exactly the same
but it is a similar scheme.

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:38.020
And so we're hoping that in
the near future we'll be able to

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:41.860
integrate this kind of
thing directly into Shinylive to

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:43.600
create a Shinylive for R,

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:48.760
which will take the complication
of deploying apps like this

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:50.230
away so that

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:56.050
the general user will be able
to use Shinylive to deploy R

00:10:56.050 --> 00:10:58.870
Shiny apps in this way rather
than having to learn the

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:02.140
intricacies of how to use
a JavaScript service worker or

00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:04.180
deploy their own
applications in this way.

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:06.160
I do have an example.

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:09.790
So this is a Shiny app
running directly in the web browser.

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:12.445
And you can confirm for
yourself that this really is running

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:14.800
entirely client side,

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:18.080
because that output at the
bottom of the screen is the

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:19.310
output from an R console

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:21.605
running a Shiny server.

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:24.230
So that output is directly

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:26.975
from the Shiny server itself.

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:29.120
And I could disconnect
the network,

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:31.550
I could turn the internet off
and this would still work.

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:37.130
The code for that demo is
available on GitHub. So if you

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:41.660
are a JavaScript developer and you
know about web workers and service workers,

00:11:42.710 --> 00:11:45.320
you can read a little more
about how this works in a bit

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:47.315
more detail on that
on that GitHub page.

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:50.450
And it also talks about
some of the requirements for deployment,

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:54.290
particularly when it comes
to setting special HTTP headers

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:56.000
for cross-origin isolation.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:59.720
And this is something that's
required to get webR to run

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:02.330
in this environment.

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:07.370
So that's everything I wanted
to talk about R packages.

00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:11.660
So do visit the REPL app at
the URL at the top of the screen

00:12:11.660 --> 00:12:14.180
there and try this out,
load some packages,

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:16.595
and see for yourself which
ones work and which ones don't.

00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:19.640
And if you do want to
create your own webR apps,

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:21.905
it's available on
NPM or via CDN.

00:12:22.730 --> 00:12:25.790
And the documentation
is at that URL there,

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:28.250
which is well worth looking
through if you are wanting to

00:12:28.250 --> 00:12:30.980
integrate webR into your
own web applications.

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:32.080
If you

00:12:32.540 --> 00:12:37.130
do any interesting projects with webR
and want to share them, please do

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:39.260
feel free to email me.

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:40.940
And my email is shown
on the screen there.

00:12:40.940 --> 00:12:42.695
And I'd love to see
what kind of projects

00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:45.890
you create with webR.
Thank you very much. [MUSIC PLAYING]

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:46.790
PLAYING]

